⛔ THIS COMMIT MESSAGE IS DAMAGED, AND BY THE AUTHOR'S OWN SHELL. Added minutes later.

The paragraph beginning "Cure that worked" reads:

    parse the language — walk from  to the first  and test THAT block.

Two gaps. It should read:

    parse the language — walk from `theorem <name>` to the first `:=` and test THAT block.

WHAT HAPPENED: I used `git commit -m "…"` with a DOUBLE-QUOTED string containing backticks.
Bash performed COMMAND SUBSTITUTION on them before git ever saw the text — it tried to run
`theorem <name>` and `:=` as commands, printed "command not found" to my terminal, and
substituted EMPTY STRINGS into the message. The commit succeeded; the message did not.

⇒ THE DAMAGE LANDED EXACTLY ON THE CURE — the one sentence a future reader would want, deleted
by the mechanism the sentence is about.

⭐ THE RULE, TIGHTENED: every commit message goes through `git commit -F -` with a QUOTED
heredoc (<<'EOF'), never `-m`. My own card says a string reaching a shell must never be
interpolated; I had been obeying it by habit with heredocs and deviated for one "short" message.
The safe form is not a preference, it is the only form — and the tell that I had left it is that
bash printed errors DURING A SUCCESSFUL COMMIT, which is a combination I should read as
corruption rather than noise.

📌 A RECEIPT COVERS BYTES SENT, NEVER BYTES INTENDED. `git commit` exited 0 and pushed clean; the
verification that mattered was reading the message back, which is what found this.
